WEBVTT

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.160
And I think taken together, that gives us confidence that the study

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:03.780
is performing.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:10.600
As expected, in terms of the differences between the active and the control arm.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:17.100
But not more we can say at this point until we ultimately read out the trial.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.660
I think for us, clearly with Remy Bruteneb, in addition to looking at annualized relapse

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:28.720
rate and MRI performance, how it performs in disability progression will be important

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.000
to understand is Remy something that would be used?

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:43.660
In a setting post the B-cell antibodies, or could it be used in a setting in line or ahead of the B-cell antibodies?

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.160
And this will all be data driven.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.660
And obviously, once we see that data, we'll be able to provide better guidance on that use.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.600
But that's probably about as much as we can provide at this point.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:55.900
Thank you.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:57.900
Thank you.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.600
Your next question comes from the line of Richard Foster from JP Morgan.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.440
Please go ahead.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.400
Hi, thanks for taking my question.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.400
Just another question on Pella Carson.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:14.800
We've seen some other cardiovascular trials recently suffer from high levels of drop-ins

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:15.900
of existing therapies.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:22.600
So, wondering how you've controlled for that in the Horizon trial, just thinking about

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:29.100
it in relation to, I suppose, PCSK9s, but also for GLP-1s and SGLT2s, given the 25%

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.600
of patients that are diabetics.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.100
So, how should we think about that level of use?

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:33.600
And potentially, the effect of that?

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.400
And potentially, the impact on any benefits of Pella Carson?

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:38.600
Thanks very much.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.200
Yeah, thanks, Richard.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.800
I mean, it is important to know we study Pella Carson on top of optimized background lipid

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.500
lowering therapy.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.300
I mean, our current estimate is that the number of patients who were on incretin-based

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.900
therapies in the study is less than 10%.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.200
I mean, we estimate it to be around 6%.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:03.300
So, we don't believe that if there was any effect from those medicines.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.900
In the setting, that we wouldn't expect that to impact the results here.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:14.400
So, when we look at it overall, it's as we, more or less as we planned in terms of background

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.400
therapy.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.000
And so, we don't think that will be a major swing factor, at least based on what we can

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.000
see so far.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.460
Thank you.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:24.460
Did you?

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:25.460
Okay.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:26.460
Yeah, go ahead.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:27.460
Go ahead, Richard.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:28.460
No, it was just on PCSK9s.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:29.460
Just one quick follow-up.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:30.460
Was that controlled as well?

